{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The problem asks for evidence that would distinguish reliability gain from mere compliance 'within a single evaluation.' SRC_001's mechanism requires inserting a checklist 'decoy' or 'negative trigger' that is NOT already part of the task's natural checklist. That means the evidentiary test it prescribes cannot be read off the same evaluation the panel is being judged on—it requires the evaluator to have designed a separate trap condition. But the distinguishing evidence in the problem is what a genuine evaluator observes *within* the panel's actual performance on the task, not whether a test-engineer happened to embed adversarial traps. So SRC_001 fails the problem's 'within a single evaluation' constraint: its mechanism is only applicable when the evaluation has been pre-instrumented with synthetic negative constraints, which is an extra-experimental condition the problem never grants.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism hinges on the expert using a 'high-dimensional mental model' to perform 'exception handling' when a rule doesn't apply. But the problem context fixes the outcome as objectively verifiable; the question is comparative reliability *on average* over such tasks. SRC_002 provides no causal account for why the expert's exception-handling is calibrated rather than rationalized post hoc: a high-dimensional mental model can just as easily license idiosyncratic overrides that happen to be wrong, and without a specified mechanism that ties the override to the verifiable signal (rather than to priors or preference), there is no reason to expect the expert's edge-case judgments to be more reliable than a checklist's literal ones. The claim is asserted as a mechanism but the actual causal link from 'high-dimensional model' to 'correct exception' is missing; the same model could systematically mis-fire on exactly the non-standard cases the target says it handles.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's core mechanism is the premise that expert intuition is 'actually the rapid execution of a highly optimized internal checklist,' so an explicit panel checklist is 'a low-resolution version of this.' But the problem asks the target to argue for or against the comparative-reliability claim and to identify distinguishing evidence; SRC_003 provides no mechanism by which to demonstrate the equivalence it presupposes. If the expert's internal process is unobservable and the target offers no operationalization (no commitment to an evaluation that would show the internal checklist contains the same information as the explicit one), then the claim that the panel provides 'no reliability gain' is unfalsifiable within a single evaluation. Worse, the target's own countercondition concedes the expert's internal checklist can be 'corrupted by cognitive biases,' which is a direct causal pathway by which an explicit, shared checklist could be *more* reliable precisely because it forbids biases the expert's compressed process encodes. SRC_003 never reconciles that concession with its no-gain conclusion.","counterexample":null}]}